<urn:atm:triage:finding/S7-CI-001>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "S7-CI-001" ;
  triage:title "Linux CI panic — read_mail_updates_sidecar_for_ulid_authored_message_without_mutating_inbox: timestamp collision causes non-deterministic sort" ;
  triage:description "crates/atm-core/tests/mailbox_locking.rs:759 (feature/pS-s7-queue-query) — Fixture::new() writes an 'arch pending' message whose timestamp is set to IsoTimestamp::from_datetime(Utc::now()) at fixture construction time. send_mail is then called in the test body, generating a 'hello sidecar' message whose timestamp is extracted from its ULID (millisecond precision). On Linux CI, Fixture::new() and send_mail execute within the same millisecond, so both messages share the same IsoTimestamp when compared. sort_and_limit_selected(&mut selected, Some(1)) sorts descending by timestamp and falls through to a UUID-v4 tiebreak on message_id (right.envelope.message_id.cmp(&left.envelope.message_id)). Because UUIDs are random, ~50% of tiebreak outcomes sort 'arch pending' ahead of 'hello sidecar', so limit=1 returns 'arch pending' as outcome.message. The assertion outcome.message.as_ref().is_some_and(|m| m.envelope.text == \"hello sidecar\") then fails. macOS and Windows do not reproduce because their CI runners are slower or the timing window is wider, so the ULID timestamp is reliably newer. Fix: construct the 'arch pending' fixture message with an explicitly past timestamp (e.g. Utc::now() - Duration::seconds(1)) so 'hello sidecar' always wins the sort deterministically. Same root cause as S7-FTQ-001." ;
  triage:category "CI" ;
  triage:severity "blocking" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-S" ;
  triage:repeatable false ;
  triage:sweepScope "file" ;
  triage:status "open" ;
  triage:dispatchReady true ;
  triage:triagedAt "2026-05-09T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/S7-CI-001/s7/1> ;
  triage:openOn <urn:atm:triage:worktree/feature-pS-s7-queue-query/9c01bca> .

